---
import Base from '../../../layouts/Base.astro';
---

<Base
  title="Knock Em' Down Detailing · Brett Boggs"
  description="Off a Squarespace template and onto something that sells the work."
>
  <article>
    <header class="page-head">
      <p class="eyebrow">Websites · 2026</p>
      <h1>Knock Em' Down Detailing</h1>
      <p class="lede">Off a Squarespace template and onto something that sells the work.</p>
    </header>

    <figure
      class="bleed"
      data-plate
      data-title="Knock Em' Down Detailing"
      tabindex="0"
      role="button"
      aria-label="Open the picture at full size"
    >
      <img
        src="/peek/ked-detailing-lg.webp"
        alt="The Knock Em Down wordmark over a foam-covered Porsche badge."
        width="1440"
        height="960"
        loading="eager"
      />
    </figure>

    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        Jacob details cars where they sit, mostly around St. Louis, including for a Porsche
        dealership. The site he had was a Squarespace template that never got finished. The page
        title still said "Your Site Title". The store sold nothing. The copy read like it came out
        of a chatbot, because it had.
      </p>
      <p>
        The problem was not that it looked bad. It was that none of the good stuff was on it. He
        shoots a foam cannon over a G-Wagon at a stone estate at dusk and none of that footage was
        anywhere. He has twenty-five five-star reviews and the page showed four testimonials that
        nobody had written. He details boats, which is in his registered business name, and the
        site had never mentioned it.
      </p>
      <p>
        So the rebuild was mostly about getting real material in front of people. The hero is his
        own 4K footage. The reviews are his actual ones, with names, linked back to where they
        live. The type is a condensed grotesque that can carry the brand at poster scale, and
        because the business is called Knock Em Down, the wordmark gets knocked over as you
        scroll past it.
      </p>
      <p>
        Built in Astro and hosted for nothing. Every URL the old site published still resolves, so
        the blog posts he already ranks for did not go dark. He can add merch and write posts
        himself through a visual editor that commits to the repo, so nothing routine needs me.
      </p>
    </div>

    <p class="prose">
      <a href="/ked/">Open the site</a> — the full build, running here while the domain moves.
    </p>

    <a class="back-link" href="/work/websites/">All websites</a>
  </article>
</Base>
